d coordinates: e8 is Black king, h5 is four squares away diagonally from e8? Actually h5 to e8: h5-g6-f7-e8, that’s a straight diagonal? Yes h5-g6-f7-e8 is a diagonal of length 4, but if pawn is on g6, it blocks at g6, so no check. But if f7 pawn is gone, the diagonal h5-e8 is clear except maybe pieces? Let’s check: h5 to e8: squares g6, f7, e8. If pawn moves from f7 to g6, f7 is empty; so h5 to e8 passes through g6 (occupied by pawn) then f7 (empty) then e8 (king). The pawn at g6 blocks the check at g6, so Qh5+ not possible. So safe.  \n\nThe bigger difference: f7-g6 opens f-file for rook h8 maybe later, but also removes pawn from defending e6; after h7-g6, e6 is defended by f7 and d7 (if needed). Actually e6 is currently empty. So both are fine.  \n\nh7-g6 seems more solid because it keeps f7 pawn guarding e6 and the center. Also after h7-g6, if White moves other knight to attack something, Black can develop quickly with Nf6 possibly to e4 later or develop bishops.  \n\nBut also in the valid moves list, f7-g6 is listed, h7-g6 is listed, so both are valid captures.  \n\nReason:  \nTaking the knight for free is the best tactical decision. Since White’s knight on g6 is undefended and attacked three times, capturing with h7-g6 keeps pawn structure more solid and maintains central pawn on f7. It also doesn’t weaken king safety as much as moving f7 would. It’s a straightforward material gain with no downside.  \n\nAnswer: P: h7-g6'}
