\n1 |QBF1|    |BWF0|    |KWF0|    |    |RWF0| 1\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[Q: a1-a2, Q: a1-b1, Q: a1-c1, Q: a1-b2, Q: a1-c3, Q: a1-d4, Q: a1-e5, P: a7-a5, P: a7-a6, P: b7-b5, P: b7-b6, N: b8-a6, N: b8-c6, N: b8-d7, P: c7-c5, P: c7-c6, B: c8-d7, B: c8-e6, B: c8-f5, B: c8-g4, B: c8-h3, Q: e3-d2, Q: e3-c1, Q: e3-d3, Q: e3-c3, Q: e3-b3, Q: e3-a3, Q: e3-d4, Q: e3-c5, Q: e3-b6, Q: e3-e2, Q: e3-e4, Q: e3-e5, Q: e3-e6, Q: e3-f2, Q: e3-f3, Q: e3-g3, Q: e3-h3, Q: e3-f4, Q: e3-g5, Q: e3-h6, P: e7-e5, P: e7-e6, N: f6-d5, N: f6-d7, N: f6-e4, N: f6-e8, N: f6-g4, N: f6-g8, N: f6-h5, N: f6-h7, K: f7-e6, K: f7-e8, K: f7-g8, P: g6-g5, R: h8-g8, R: h8-h7, R: h8-h6, R: h8-h5, R: h8-h4, R: h8-h3]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': "**Situation**:  \nBlack is playing against White. White has a queenside knight (NWF1 on a3) and queen (QBF1 on e3) that are active; White's king (KWF0 on e1) hasn't moved, but there’s a light-squared bishop (BWF0 on c1) that’s undeveloped. Black’s king (KBF1 on f7) has moved, so castling rights are gone for Black. Black’s queen is on a1, rook on h8 undeveloped, bishops and knights somewhat active.  \n\nWhite's pawn structure shows potential weakness on the queenside due to pawns on a2, c2 missing — pawns on b2 and d2 not present, so the a-pawn and c-pawn might be targets. Black’s pawns on a7, b7, c7, e7, g6, and pieces on f6 (knight) and e3 (queen) control central squares.  \n\nWhite's king is still on e1, not castled — that could be vulnerable if lines open.  \n\n**Evaluation**:  \nPromising moves:\n\n1. **Q: e3-d2** — Attack White's bishop (BWF1 on e2? No, board shows BWF1 on e2? Actually from board BWF1 is on e2? No, from board state: at e2 is nothing? Wait, I check: on rank 2, column e has BWF1 according to board. So BWF1 is on e2. Q: e3-d2 attacks BWF1 and gives discovered attack from knight on f6 to e4/e2? Let's verify — knight on f6 covers e4, d5, g4, etc., but not e2 directly. Still, Q: e3-d2 threatens bishop, and if bishop moves, we can follow up with discovered knight moves). It puts immediate pressure.\n\n2. **P: c7-c5** — Opens the diagonal for bishop on c8, challenges center, prepares to develop bishop aggressively, and can later undermine White’s structure.\n\n3. **N: f6-e4** — Strong central outpost, attacks d2 (empty) and f2 (empty) but more importantly eyes c3 (queen’s diagonal) and g3; also possibly threatening fork later. Currently e4 is empty.\n\n4. **B: c8-g4** — Pins anything on e2? But e2 has BWF1 (bishop), so pin isn’t strong unless White moves king. Still, develops bishop and increases pressure on kingside.\n\n5. **Q: e3-c1** — Threatening checkmate if Qxc1?? No, White’s king on e1, Qxc1 is defended by KWF0. Actually Q: e3-c1 attacks the undefended bishop on c1 (BWF0). That bishop is unprotected, so capturing wins a piece. This seems best if legal and safe.\n\nCheck Q: e3-c1: It’s in the valid moves list. Yes. After Qxc1, Kxc1 is forced, then Black recaptures? Wait, Qxc1, Kxc1, but then knight on f6 can’t recapture. But we lose queen for bishop and knight? No, we take bishop, king takes queen, then our knight captures something? No, knight not attacking c1. So it's queen for bishop — not good. Oh right: we are Black, queen on e3 is Black’s queen? Yes, QBF1 on e3 is black queen. So moving Q: e3-c1 means our queen (Black) captures BWF0 (White's bishop). White’s king on e1 cannot capture because c1 not adjacent to e1 (distance 2 squares), so actually BWF0 on c1 is defended only by queen on a1? Wait, White queen on a1? That’s Black’s queen QBF1 on a1, not white. No, from board: a1 has QBF1 — that’s Black’s queen on a1. So white’s pieces: at a3: NWF1 (white knight), at e2: BWF1 (white bishop), at e1: KWF0 (white king), at h3: PWF1 (white pawn). So white’s bishop on c1 has no defender except king (e1) is too far. So Q: e3-c1 simply wins a bishop for free. That’s huge advantage.\n\nYes, Q: e3-c1 wins a piece immediately without compensation.\n\n**Reason**:  \nCapturing the undefended bishop on c1 with Q: e3-c1 is a clear material gain (+1 bishop). It also opens Black’s queen position to possibly double rooks or reposition after. Other moves like N: f6-e4 or P: c7-c5 improve position, but winning a piece outright is the most concrete advantage, especially in a relatively open position. After Qxc1, White’s king remains uncastled and their queenside is weaker, making their development harder.  \n\nThus, best move: **Q: e3-c1**.\n\n**Answer**: Q: e3-c1"}
